import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BN3Eol-0.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"UXD-020 — /explore readability: one 12 px label, one ring, one placement pass","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"uxs/decisions/UXD-020-explore-readability-pins-rings-labels.md","filePath":"uxs/decisions/UXD-020-explore-readability-pins-rings-labels.md"}'),r={name:"uxs/decisions/UXD-020-explore-readability-pins-rings-labels.md"};function i(n,e,l,d,c,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="uxd-020-—-explore-readability-one-12-px-label-one-ring-one-placement-pass" tabindex="-1">UXD-020 — /explore readability: one 12 px label, one ring, one placement pass <a class="header-anchor" href="#uxd-020-—-explore-readability-one-12-px-label-one-ring-one-placement-pass" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;UXD-020 — /explore readability: one 12 px label, one ring, one placement pass&quot;">​</a></h1><ul><li><strong>Status</strong>: accepted (built; the E2 probe table and the shots are the lead&#39;s to run, see Verification)</li><li><strong>Date</strong>: 2026-11-12 (proposed and decided)</li><li><strong>Decider</strong>: the operator, 2026-11-12 — every open question of <code>docs/wip/2026-11-12-slice-C2-brief.md</code> §11 approved as recommended</li><li><strong>Answers</strong>: E2, SN1, MW1, MW2, MW4, MW6 of the UX feedback plan; slice C2</li><li><strong>Builds on</strong>: <a href="./UXD-019-select-equals-focus">UXD-019</a> (C1: the selection state and the ring for every kind in the solar scene; C2 does not re-implement it), <a href="./UXD-002-type-scale-and-the-11px-floor">UXD-002</a> (the 11 px floor; ends its &quot;3D sprite labels are slice C2&quot; deferral), <a href="./UXD-001-ui-accent-blue-replaces-teal">UXD-001</a> (no teal or cyan in rings and states), <a href="./UXD-004-one-outputpass-per-composer">UXD-004</a> (the look after the OutputPass is accepted; global exposure is not changed here)</li><li><strong>Changes</strong>: <code>src/lib/universe/scene-pins.ts</code> (new, with its test), <code>scene-pin-sprites.ts</code> (new), the seven pin scenes (<code>neighborhood-</code>, <code>milky-way-</code>, <code>local-group-</code>, <code>local-sheet-</code>, <code>virgo-</code>, <code>laniakea-</code>, <code>cosmic-web-scene.ts</code>) and <code>deep-sky-scene.ts</code>, <code>src/lib/three/explore-scene-host.ts</code> (the hover ring, the 2D text, the probes, the viewport height passed to every <code>update</code>), <code>explore-solar-scene.ts</code> (hover ring, element lifts), <code>scripts/readability-probe.mjs</code>, <code>scripts/readability-before.sh</code> and <code>scripts/lib/readability-contrast.mjs</code> (new), <code>tests/e2e/explore-readability.spec.ts</code> (new); <a href="./../UXS-014">UXS-014</a>, <a href="./../UXS-016">UXS-016</a>, <code>docs/adr/TA.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <a href="./UXD-002-type-scale-and-the-11px-floor">UXD-002</a>, <code>src/lib/universe/README.md</code>, <code>scripts/README.md</code></li></ul><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The labels were too big where they overlapped and too small where they did not: the Milky Way&#39;s were 16.8 px at a 768 px viewport, the neighbourhood&#39;s 9.7 px (below the floor). Eleven Milky Way labels, and 2 to 5 at every outer scale, were placed with no overlap pass (only the neighbourhood had one). A hovered galaxy&#39;s label jumped, because five scenes put the label above the hover-grown glow. The arm labels were pickable and never showed it. The &quot;you are here&quot; ring was a different size and behaviour at each scale (screen-constant at five, world-scaled at the Local Group, gold at the neighbourhood) and teal at six. The light-year rings were 1 px lines at opacity 0.2 in four hues, with a literal &quot;ly&quot; and a label that collided with a star name. Evidence: the brief&#39;s §0 to §3.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>One label size.</strong> Text is 12 px (<code>--size-meta</code>) in CSS pixels at every scale and viewport; the sprite is sized in pixels from the VIEW-SPACE depth of the pin (<code>viewDepth</code>: how far in front of the image plane it is, which is what three&#39;s sprite shader projects with), the field of view and the viewport height (<code>screenPxToWorld</code>), not as a fraction of the distance and not from the Euclidean distance: a pin 40 degrees off the view axis drew 1 / cos 40 = 1.3 times too big that way (a 12 px label read 15.6 px, a 44 px ring 57 px) and its overlap rectangle was 1.3 times too small. The 2D view&#39;s canvas text is 11 px (the floor) at every zoom, with alphas of 0.58 or more. <code>scene-pins.ts</code> holds the constants and the maths.</li><li><strong>One ring.</strong> A thin annulus, 44 px across, 1.5 px stroke, screen-constant, on every pin of the seven scenes, on the deep-sky glints and on the label point of every Milky Way arm. Hover and selection show it at full opacity in <code>#889fe7</code> (<code>--color-state</code>). The anchor ring (&quot;you are here&quot;: the Sun, the Milky Way in the Local Group, the Local Group in the Local Sheet and Virgo, the Virgo Supercluster in Laniakea, Laniakea in the Cosmic web, Sol in the neighbourhood) is gold <code>#ffcf8f</code> at all seven levels and stays up at opacity 0.55; hovering or selecting it makes it full. Scene code cannot read a CSS variable: the blue is a literal that mirrors the token (<code>RING_BLUE</code>, as <code>HOVER_BLUE</code> in <code>surface-markers.ts</code> does). The solar scene&#39;s selection ring (C1) is the same blue; the planet or small body under the cursor gets the same outline, dimmer. The scenes take both ids (<code>highlight(hovered, selected)</code>): hovering another pin leaves the selected pin its ring and its forced label (the deep-sky layer has a ring for each).</li><li><strong>One placement pass</strong> (<code>placeLabels</code>, pure, in <code>scene-pins.ts</code>, moved out of the neighbourhood). Each frame the candidate labels are placed in priority order; one that overlaps a placed label is hidden and returns when space frees. Hovered and selected labels are always shown and placed first. Priority: hovered/selected, the anchor, headliners, the rest; the Milky Way&#39;s order is the Sun, Sagittarius A*, the headliner globulars, the Magellanic Clouds, the arms; the neighbourhood&#39;s light-year ring labels come last. Equal ranks resolve by the slot&#39;s creation <code>order</code>, so the result is the same every frame. A hidden arm label&#39;s sprite is not a pick target; the arm&#39;s ring is, hidden label or not (the arm is the object, the label is decoration), and hovering it forces the label back.</li><li><strong>A steady label offset.</strong> The label sits above the pin by the STEADY glow size and the ring (<code>labelOffset</code>), never the hover-grown one; hovering does not move it (MW2, also the deep-sky glint, whose label is no longer a child of the scaled glint). The lift runs along the camera&#39;s screen-up (<code>liftOnScreen</code>), not world Y, so it is the same on screen in every pose; along world Y it would shrink to nothing when the camera looks straight down.</li><li><strong>Light-year rings</strong> are four <code>Line2</code> lines of 2 px at opacity 0.55 on a ramp from <code>#889fe7</code> to white, with the localised unit (<code>m.unit_ly_value</code>, 14 locales, no new key) and a dark halo behind the text, in the placement pass at the lowest priority.</li><li><strong>No teal in rings, anchor glows or labels.</strong> The anchor glows of the four shell scenes are neutral blue-white; the Milky Way Sun and the Local Group headliner labels are the neutral label colour; the Milky Way lens labels and rotation-curve rings take the neutral blue-white and <code>#889fe7</code>. The galaxy glows by kind keep their colours (data colours, <code>a3-keep</code>).</li><li><strong>E2 is measured, then lifted by element, never by exposure.</strong> A probe screenshots each level (and the solar system flown in to Jupiter, where the moon orbits show) and measures the WCAG contrast of the rendered pixels of the orbit lines, the two belts and the rings (target 3:1) and again under a 12 % white veil (target 2:1), at device pixel ratio 1 and 2. Each element is judged by its contrast MINUS a negative control (the same points shifted 8 px off the element), so a bright starfield or glow cannot fake a pass; the verdict is on the p50, the p10 is printed. Global exposure (1.15) and the hemisphere light are not touched. Lifted, as starting values to be confirmed by the probe: planet orbit lines 0.25 to 0.4, small-body orbits 0.22 to 0.35, the belts 0.5 / 0.4 to 0.65 / 0.55.</li></ol><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Names are shorter on screen at the outer scales (12 px against 14 to 20 px) and longer at the neighbourhood (12 px against 9.7 px); the pass hides the ones that collide, so a crowded view shows fewer names and the rest reveal on hover. Every pin shows a 44 px ring on hover and selection, and the arms are highlighted like the pins. Pick targets are NOT the rings everywhere: the ring is the pick target in the neighbourhood (named stars and Sol) and on the Milky Way arm label points; in the other pin scenes and for the deep-sky glints the pick target is still the glow or glint sprite. Making every ring a pick target was left out because the 48 px ring sprites of neighbouring pins overlap in the dense Virgo and Laniakea fields and a sprite hit is ordered by camera distance, not by distance from the cursor (reasoned, not measured). The neighbourhood&#39;s pick area grew: the 48 px ring sprite (<code>RING_SPRITE_PX</code>) replaces the old halo of 0.04 of the camera distance, about 35 px at a 900 px viewport (0.04 / (2 tan 27.5 degrees) x 900), so the area is about 1.9 times larger there and neighbouring named stars compete for the pointer a little more. The 2D view&#39;s labels lost their world scale: they do not grow with the zoom and they take part in the pass; the Sun&#39;s caption and a planet&#39;s name start past the drawn edge of the body (the core, Saturn&#39;s ring, the selection ring), and a small body&#39;s name past its dot and a right-pointing comet tail. Not covered: the lens overlay labels of the Milky Way (world-sized teaching text), <code>iconic-trajectory</code> labels (done in C7: <a href="./UXD-026-iconic-missions-legend-and-milestone-boxes">UXD-026</a>), the Worlds&#39; site tags, <code>fly-frame-runner.ts</code> and <code>three-label.ts</code> (not measured against the floor), the ruler (C4), the solar scene&#39;s own sprite labels in <code>explore-scene-host.ts</code>.</p><p>Follow-up (2026-11-12, operator&#39;s device screenshots): the Milky Way opened with two of the four arm names off screen on a 16:9 laptop and every arm name and the Sun off screen on a phone. The arm label anchors (<code>milky-way-schematic.json</code>, schematic like every position there) moved from the disc edge to 4–9 kpc, placed so no arm name collides with a pin label at 1280, 1470, 2560 and on a phone (portrait, landscape), and a portrait window enters farther out (<code>MW_ENTRY_CAM_R_PORTRAIT</code>, 2.0× against 1.12×), as the Local Group already did. Labels beside the INDEX pull tab flip inward as at the screen edge (<code>setLabelLeftInset</code>).</p><h2 id="verification" tabindex="-1">Verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Unit: <code>src/lib/universe/scene-pins.test.ts</code> (placement and its tie-break, offset, ring state, px-to-world, ramp, and sizes 40 degrees off the view axis checked against a projected point pair, not against <code>worldPerPx</code>), <code>neighborhood-labels.test.ts</code>, <code>scripts/lib/readability-contrast.test.ts</code> (including the starfield control). E2: <code>tests/e2e/explore-readability.spec.ts</code> (label size from the projected label rectangle, overlap of the drawn rectangles, every arm ring pickable with its label hidden or not, a selection kept while another pin is hovered). The probe, at both pixel ratios: <code>node scripts/readability-probe.mjs --base=&lt;url&gt; --dpr=1</code> and <code>--dpr=2</code>. Its &quot;before&quot; numbers come from a worktree at HEAD with the four pre-slice opacities put back (<code>bash scripts/readability-before.sh &lt;dir&gt;</code>, see <code>scripts/README.md</code>); only the solar-system rows have one. The slice&#39;s check script (<code>docs/wip/2026-11-12-slice-C2-brief.md</code> §6) prints <code>C2-CHECK violations: 0</code>.</p>',11)])])}const b=t(r,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,b as default};
